welcome back to cbs mornings we are  remembering the life and career of nba  hall of famer bill russell the boston  celtics legend died peacefully yesterday  he was 88 years old russell won 11 nba  championships and five mvp awards and  let's not forget he was also the nba's  first black head coach  he was not only a champion on the court  but also a champion of civil rights  marching with the reverend dr martin  luther king jr in the 1960s we spoke  with russell late last year in one of  his last and final tv interviews  [Music]  basketball hadn't seen anything like  bill russell before  when russell jumped russell soared  the kid from monroe louisiana who nobody  wanted on their team because of the  color of his skin he redefined what the  sport could be and eventually ended up  being the teammate no one could play  without bill this must been a great win  for you jack  russell catapulted the celtics and the  city of boston skyward in the 1950s and  60s winning titles and breaking barriers  at a time when keeping your dignity as a  black man in america was easier said  than done so we're sitting here in  boston  at the garden you helped bring glory  here  11 championships here what does it feel  like to be here  on this court  i never was uh  comfortable playing for the celtics  because the number one paper was the  boston herald and they didn't like the  idea  of the nba team having black players you  brought championships here in a time  when it was hard to be a black man was  it difficult  to be celebrated on the court  but then off of it  not treated equal  first of all i  was never separated on the court when i  got there was the star was bob cousy  watch boston's bob cruzy no matter what  i did because it was a star bill russell  another celtic caps it in i remember i  had a game where i had 25 points 25  rebounds 10 blocks and  boston harold said i was lucky i got to  play with cozy  i'm not a basketball player i am a man  who plays basketball bill russell the  man is someone who stood up for the  rights and dignity of all men  bill russell wasn't just an icon for  basketball fans  to rally behind on the floor of the  boston garden on any given night nine  top negro athletes meet with cassius  clay  he was an icon for black america using  his profile to highlight racism and  inequality  here with muhammad ali and other top  black athletes in 1967 backing ali's  decision refusing the vietnam war draft  at the march alongside dr king in  washington do you remember in kentucky  where you and your teammates set out  because they refused  service at a restaurant  yeah  at that time why did you feel like it  was important to do that well things are  part of our preparations for the game  the team would have  dinner together and i had a couple of  teammates from kentucky  and they were going to have a  separate pregame meal  so i told the coach i'm not going to  play tonight he said why not  i said cause of that  later that month this letter found its  way to russell's mailbox it was from  jackie robinson who broke major league  baseball's color barrier more than a  decade earlier thanking russell for  boycotting the game in kentucky jackie  was uh much more to him than most people  do  they just knew he was the first black  player in the major leagues  i mean they were good okay  but they didn't know he was uh probably  the most intelligent baseball player in  baseball wow and jackie would not take  anything off anybody i tried to get as  many as him i couldn't get there  which championship was the most special  to you was it the first one  or was it the last one the first year i  was player coach and we won the  championship first black coach in the  nba  is that right yeah  and you won as a player and a coach yeah  well you understand some my motivation  was to make myself acceptable to my  folks i never felt like i was making the  contribution  i was just doing the best i could  former president obama paid tribute to  russell on social media he wrote as tall  as bill russell stood his legacy rises  far higher both as a player and a person  perhaps more than anyone else bill knew  what it took to win and what it took to  lead  on the court he was the greatest  champion in basketball history off of it  he was a civil rights trailblazer those  are perfectly put words by former  president obama um you know he's such a  legend he accomplished so much on the  court  but  that pales in comparison to who he was  off of it right  and when at the time when players like  bill russell had a lot more to lose  it's much easier now i would argue in  2022 agreed than it was back when bill  russell and jackie robinson and muhammad  ali and kareem abdul-jabbar were playing  and michael jordan himself released a  statement when he said he set an example  for every black player that came into  the league including me michael jordan  and not just the players i look at this  table right now and i think if not for  people like bill russell yeah you know  there was a time we're putting two three  african-americans on television national  television on a morning show just you're  right we wouldn't be talking about it  and i have to say this and i'll be  honest when these young players come in  the league and they're blessed with  everything they're making millions  they're praised for what they do in  their sport  and they refuse to use their voice and  their platform for what's right  i think it's quite cowardly  because you had men and women doing it  in a time where it was tough because of  sometimes their life was at risk right  just because they stood up for what they  believed in so a lot of young players  they should look at individuals like  bill russell he will be remembered and  we'll talk about lead from him a little  bit later in the show as well
 

